<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afonso I of Kongo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1509">1509</a>–<a href="/years/1543">1543</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Afonso I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afonso I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, also known as Nzinga Mbemba, was king of the Kongo Empire from 1509 to 1543. A committed Catholic, he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, literacy and ties with Portugal, while trying to strengthen royal authority. His surviving letters to the Portuguese crown protest the damage caused by the growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo.jpg" alt="Afonso I of Kongo" width="293" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Affricoll9 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manikongo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Afonso, born Mvemba a Nzinga around 1456, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nzinga-a-nkuwu">Nzinga a Nkuwu</a>, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> who took the name João I on conversion. As a young man he ruled the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsundi">Nsundi</a>, in the northeast of the kingdom. The first Portuguese reached the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Mbanza Kongo</a>, in 1491, and Mvemba took quickly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, took the name Afonso and studied for ten years with Portuguese priests. Around 1495 his father turned against Christianity and pushed the priests out of the capital, but Afonso welcomed them into Nsundi.</p><p>After João died in 1506 Afonso, whose mother had kept the news secret while he gathered followers in the capital, prevailed against his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpanzu-a-kitima">Mpanzu a Kitima</a> in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Mbanza Kongo</a>. Kongo was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elective-monarchy">elective monarchy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Afonso centralised government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">Mbanza Kongo</a>, curbed the rural nobility and expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>'s borders to the south and east; captives were enslaved, and conquered lands paid tribute in iron, palm cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the royal faith, paid for the church from taxes and founded schools; by 1516 more than 1,000 students attended the royal school.</p><p>Trade with Portugal grew, and with it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, which Portuguese demand from the colony on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> drove up. By the 1510s Afonso received Portuguese military help in exchange for a share of the captives of his wars. By the mid-1520s nobles were illegally exporting slaves and even kidnapping freeborn Kongolese. In 1526 Afonso wrote to the king of Portugal: &quot;Each day the traders are kidnapping our people.&quot; He threatened to end the trade and set up a committee to judge the legality of enslavement, which for a time slowed the enslavement of free Kongolese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1540 plotters, among them Portuguese residents, made an unsuccessful attempt on his life. He died around the turn of 1542 to 1543 and was succeeded by his son Pedro, who was overthrown by Afonso's grandson Diogo in 1545. Descendants of three of Afonso's daughters supplied many later kings. Almost everything known about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> in his time comes from his long series of letters to the Portuguese kings Manuel I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">João III</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afonso I of Kongo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Loango is absent from the titles of King <b>Afonso I of Kongo</b> in 1535.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1518 the ngola sent envoys to Portugal asking for missionaries, but the mission that came in 1520 achieved little, and <b>Afonso I of Kongo</b> took its priests to his own court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom expanded and held together by campaigning, particularly during the reign of <b>Afonso I</b> (r. 1509–1543), who won the throne by defeating his half-brother Mpanzu a Kitima and then sought to strengthen central authority and suppress rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese, interested above all in the slave trade, kept a peaceful and profitable relationship with the Kongo's rulers and nobles; kings such as João I and <b>Afonso I</b> studied Christianity, learned Portuguese and shared the profits of the trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Afonso I</b>, king from 1509, made Catholicism the royal faith, nobles learned to read in royal schools and took Portuguese names and, later, titles such as duke and count.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kongo">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Kongo</a></li><li><blockquote>With crusader help, <b>Afonso I</b>, the first king of Portugal, ended a siege begun on 1 July and took Lisbon from the Moors.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-25">25 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">M'banza-Kongo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-kongo">M'banza-Kongo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-6.27,14.25&amp;year=1526" aria-label="M'banza-Kongo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Afonso I of Kongo"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6199 5535h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6199 5535h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-6.27,14.25&amp;year=1526">What was here in 1526? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
